# Keep the work moving

The owner's time is the scarce thing. A row is finished when its own work is done and measured. Everything else that turns up is written down and handed on, and the row goes on.

## What belongs to the row, and what is split off

`.cursor/rules/open-ends.mdc` asks that a PR time its stages and explain its figures. That is about the code the row adds or changes. A finding about code the row did not change is another piece of work, however it was found:

1. Write what was measured on a new Issue: the numbers, what was tried, what is known and what is not. Before the Issue names a part as the cause, a bounded probe checks it (who calls the read that waits, which stage the time is in); a symptom alone is written as a symptom. Issue #328 named the engine's frames from a test that held a chunk on 15 workers, and a rule was written on it; the readers' stacks later showed the test storage holding the frame's own M4 read.
2. Add a roadmap row that names it (`.cursor/rules/defer.mdc`). Say whether it blocks the rows after it.
3. Go on with the row. The PR body points to the Issue; it does not carry the investigation. An investigation of a failure that `main` also has does not go into the row's PR comments and commits, with the code it needs; it goes to its own Issue and PR.

The test is one question: would this be there if the row had never been written?

Before filing an Issue for a failure, search the open Issues and the roadmap's rows (the rows that track open measurements above all) for the same symptom. When one exists, add the new figures to it, and offer options that start from the decisions recorded there: the owner may already have decided how failures of that kind are handled until they are explained.

## A limit on an investigation

After two attempts that change nothing, stop and decide before a third:

- The cause is known and the fix is not worth it: write the numbers and leave it.
- The cause is not known: it needs another tool (a profile, a disassembly, an allocation trace, a GPU capture), not another rewrite. If the figure is the row's own, use the tool now. If not, split it off as above.

Report to the owner at that point, in a line: what stands, what was tried, what happens next. Do not go on silently.

A test run that stops on CI and not on this machine counts its local reruns as the attempts. After two, the other tool is the dump of the stopped process on CI: the gate runs every test with a hang dump, and CI uploads `TestResults` when the gate fails or is cancelled. In Issue #260, 18 local runs (fewer cores, runs side by side) never stopped, and the first CI run with the dump showed the join that never ended.

A run that passes after a diagnostic was added is not evidence of the cause. A race comes and goes with timing, and printing each test changes the timing: in Issue #260, the CI passed six times in a row once each test was printed, and the output of the test platform looked like the cause. The stack of the dump showed a race in the worker pool.

## What comes next is decided, not read off a list

Before starting a piece of work, write one line for it: what a user of the library has after it that they do not have now. Write the same line for everything else that is open. Start the one with the strongest line. The classes, strongest first:

1. A wrong result, a red `main`, an open review finding.
2. A capability the requirements name that is missing or half done.
3. Speed or memory a user can see, with the case (data size, viewport) and the ratio named.
4. An explanation of a figure, with nothing to fix.

Within a class, work whose result can change how existing code is built (a spike, a measurement of a whole subsystem, a review of an interface) comes before work that is put on top of it.

## Long runs do not hold the working tree

A benchmark, a NativeAOT publish, a profile, or the generation of a billion-point data file keeps the tree busy for minutes.

- Run it in the background, never in the foreground with a wait loop.
- Run it on a copy of the tree (`git worktree add` for a committed state) with its own `bin/` and `obj/`. Two worktrees never share build output.
- While it runs, do what does not depend on its result. Name that work before starting the run.
- A PR's own CI is such a run. Watch it in the background and go on with the next work (the next row's facts, the Grill round) in the same turn; read its result before reporting it. After the chore PR of PR #181's retrospective the agent waited on its CI in the foreground before the Grill of row G8, and the owner called it wasted time.
- Runs that need a quiet machine (compared benchmarks) do not overlap each other.
- The local gate (`./build.ps1`, about 25 minutes) is such a run, and it needs a quiet machine too: no build, test run, or measurement beside it. A gate that ran beside GPU benchmarks failed a warm-up test once (Issue #442), and the Zero-Alloc tests count allocations that only follow a collection when another build makes garbage (`.cursor/rules/bench.mdc`). While it runs, do the work that builds nothing: write the next fix in another worktree, the documents, the PR body, the replies to a review. With the reviews of PRs #443 and #444 open together, the second PR's fixes and tests were written during the first one's gate, and built after it.
- A turn does not end only to wait for a run. The owner, 2026-11-13, when the agent had watched a long test run and done nothing else: time is finite, and there is always work that does not depend on the result. End the turn when nothing is left that can be done without the result, and say what is waited for and how long it takes.
  - Before ending a turn while a run holds the machine, list the open work that does not need the run: open review findings, the open PRs' states (`gh pr list --state merged`: a merge starts its retrospective and the cleanup of its branch), each open row's Grill frontier and the facts it needs, prototypes to write, Issues to file or answer, records to write. Each item is either started (by the agent, or by a subagent in its own worktree) or named with what blocks it. A turn ends only when every item is running or blocked. The owner, 2026-11-13 evening, after the agent ended a turn "waiting for the gate" with the R3b2 block-size sweep, the R3c measurement's preparation, and the K0b–K0e Grill facts not started, a review on PR #476 unanswered, and PRs #465, #467, and #468 merged without their retrospectives: "手を止めるなって言いませんでしたっけ？規約化したほうがいいのでは？" — this item was already written above it, and the agent still did not check it.
    The open work includes the rows after the current one (their Grill facts can be prepared), the open spike Issues (their prototypes can be written), and the open Issues of the backlog that need no decision, not only the PRs and the current row. After PR #503, the owner asked "やること無くなったの？暇なの？" with one measurement chain and one agent running, while the K0b to K0e spikes, Issue #475, and the Grill facts of rows R3e and X1 were not started.
  - The same list is checked at each break of a long piece of work inside a turn (after a gate starts, after a PR is opened, after a Grill round is recorded), not only at the end of the turn: PRs #476 and #479 were merged while the agent wrote the review answers of other PRs, and the merges went unseen for 20 minutes.
  - The breaks are fixed actions, not the agent's sense of a break: right after `gh pr create`, right after a Decision comment is posted, right after a measurement's output is read, and right after a review is answered, run `gh pr list --state merged --limit 5` and compare it with the PRs the agent knows open. PRs #480 and #487 were merged while the agent wrote reviews and Grill rounds, and went unseen for 30 minutes after the rule above was written.
  - Work split among several agents that later meet on one branch gets the shared shapes first: the signatures of the types each adds or changes, and the names of the files. Row R3b1's four agents (spec, GPU, bench, plan) met with 20 build errors, from a constructor one added a parameter to and the others called without it.
  - A script that another waits on writes its end mark into the file the waiter reads, and both name the same file and line. The R3c measurement script printed DONE to stdout while the waiting agent read its output file, which would never have got the mark.
- Work that needs no quiet machine runs side by side with the run that does: while one gate or measurement holds the machine, subagents write code, tests, and documents in worktrees of their own (told not to build), investigate an Issue by reading, and draft the next row's Issue. The machine-bound runs go one at a time; the rest is split into as many independent tracks as the open work allows, and the count of tracks does not drop to one while work is open. The owner, 2026-11-13, twice in one afternoon: "また、減ったよ。今は1本しか走っていない" — one gate ran, and the agent waited on it instead of handing the R3b1 code, Issue #445's tests, and Issue #141's reading to subagents.

A CI run started for an experiment (`gh workflow run`, `gh run rerun`) is a long run too: while it runs, go on with work that does not need its result, on another branch. Do not wait on rounds of reruns with the next row's research undone.

Before a long run, a short one: run the script on one case, and look at the output of a background run within its first minute.

A scratch variant (a source file swapped for a measurement) is built on its own worktree, never in the tree the gate runs in. A publish with `-r` still builds the referenced libraries into the tree's shared `obj/`, and a source put back with `Copy-Item` keeps its old time (`.cursor/rules/powershell-shell.mdc`), so the gate can then test the variant instead of the source.

A background run has a time limit and a watch on its output: when the output stops growing for a few minutes, the run is stopped and looked at, not waited on. While it runs, check that the output still grows. A gate that prints nothing for half an hour is stuck, and the owner should not be the one to notice.

```text
# BAD — the row's PR grows three commits that chase a Zero-Alloc failure main also has
# GOOD — the figures go to a new Issue and a roadmap row; the PR links it and goes on
```
